@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: what one host can actually carry, and why a sandbox's share is declared rather than discovered
' covers: internal/sbx/sbx.go
' covers: tests/e2e/capacity_test.go
'
' ── WHY DECLARE A SHARE AT ALL ─────────────────────────────────────────────
' The obvious objection is the right one to answer first: an OS allocates memory
' on demand and has done for fifty years, so why hand it a number up front?
'
' Because these are VMs, not processes. A sandbox is a hardware-virtualised guest
' (Virtualization.framework on macOS — sandboxd logs it as vm::macos::vm, with its
' own vCPUs and virtio-blk devices). Three things follow, and none of them apply
' to an ordinary process:
'
'   1. The size is fixed BEFORE the guest exists. A VM's memory is claimed as a
'      region at boot. There is no moment later at which the guest kernel can be
'      told it was wrong.
'   2. The guest SPENDS what it is told it has. A Linux guest sized at 12 GiB
'      grows its page cache toward 12 GiB and sets its OOM thresholds from that
'      number. An over-large limit is not idle headroom — it is an instruction.
'   3. Neither OS can see the other's picture. The host cannot read the guest's
'      working set; the guest does not know it is one of four. Neither is placed
'      to arbitrate, and the only lever the host holds is killing the whole VM.
'
' So the failure is a CLIFF, not backpressure: the host swaps until everything
' degrades together, or a VM dies on SIGKILL carrying no OOMKilled flag and no
' message — invisible in exactly the place an operator would look. See
' MemoryLimit's own note on why sbx's host-derived default cannot bind inside a
' VM-backed daemon.
'
' ── THIS IS SECOND-BEST, AND SHOULD BE LABELLED AS SUCH ────────────────────
' The first-best answer IS on-demand, and it exists: free-page reporting and
' memory ballooning let a guest hand pages back so the host can re-lend them.
' proveo declares a share because it cannot currently negotiate one, NOT because
' a static partition is the better design. If sbx exposes ballooning, the right
' move is to stop dividing and start reclaiming, and this diagram should be
' rewritten rather than tuned.
'
' ── CPU IS NOT MEMORY, AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT PARTITIONED ─────────────────
' The two knobs look symmetrical. The arguments behind them are not, and reading
' the symmetry as agreement is the mistake this section exists to stop.
'
' CPU is genuinely time-shared. An idle vCPU costs essentially nothing, the host
' scheduler DOES handle it on demand, and over-subscribing cores has no cliff —
' it costs scheduling overhead and lock contention inside guests. So the cap buys
' PREDICTABILITY (a runaway build cannot starve its neighbours) and not survival.
' Memory kills the host; CPU only makes it slow.
'
' Which is why the CPU share does NOT divide by n. A strict divide under-provisions
' by reserving capacity nobody can lend back: at n=4 on 14 cores a LONE busy
' sandbox would get 3 while 11 sat idle. The share is half the machine regardless
' of n, so every sandbox may burst into the idle time its neighbours are not using
' and the counts sum past the host on purpose. Memory must sum TO the machine;
' CPU must not.
'
' Two properties of the flag that constrain this. --cpus is a COUNT, not an
' affinity: the host scheduler places the vCPUs, so on an asymmetric machine a
' share may land wholly on efficiency cores and there is no way to ask otherwise.
' And nothing anywhere rejects the sum, which is the same blindness memory has —
' it is simply harmless here.
'
' ── WHAT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED ──────────────────────────────────────────
' Host: Apple M4 Pro, 48 GiB, 14 cores — 10 performance + 4 efficiency, no SMT,
' so hw.ncpu 14 is ALL of the machine and not half of a hyperthreaded 7.
' Daemon MemTotal: 23.5 GiB (the docker VM's share, not the host's).
'   default (no knob)        -m 12031m   x4 = 47.0 GiB     no --cpus: 14 each
'   PROVEO_SBX_INSTANCES=4   -m  6015m   x4 = 23.5 GiB     --cpus 7  (x4 = 28)
'   PROVEO_SBX_MEMORY=8g     -m  8192m   x4 = 32.0 GiB     --cpus unchanged
' Setting nothing changes nothing: the historical half stays the default, because
' a resource change nobody asked for is a regression however well-reasoned.
'
' ── THE PROBE'S FIRST RUN, AND WHAT IT CORRECTED ───────────────────────────
' 2026-08-27, M4 Pro / 48 GiB, PROVEO_SBX_MEMORY=8g, no instance count set so
' --cpus was 0 (all 14 cores each). Idle `shell` sandboxes, 20s settle:
'
'   baseline  11.1 GiB free   1.7 GiB swap
'   #1-#5      9.3 GiB free   1.7 GiB swap   (+0.0)
'   #6         8.8            1.8            (+0.1)
'   #8         8.9            2.7            (+1.0)
'   #11        8.6            4.2            (+2.5)   → stopped here
'   UPPER BOUND: 10 concurrent, and sbx never refused a launch
'
' Eleven sandboxes each ENTITLED to 8 GiB is 88 GiB of claims on a 48 GiB host,
' and it ran. Total additional consumption at #11 was ~5.0 GiB — the 2.5 GiB that
' left "free" plus the 2.5 GiB that went to swap — so an IDLE sandbox costs on the
' order of 450 MiB resident against an 8 GiB ceiling.
'
' That is the number this diagram was missing. The limit is a CEILING, not a
' reservation, and idle instances are cheap: the danger is not N sandboxes
' existing, it is several growing toward their ceilings AT ONCE. Capacity planning
' by summing limits — the arithmetic in the note below — describes the worst case
' and not the ordinary one, and treating it as a headcount would refuse runs that
' work. Which is also why the probe measures rather than calculates.
'
' Also visible: free memory PLATEAUS near 8.6-8.9 GiB from #6 while swap climbs.
' macOS holds "free" roughly constant by compressing and swapping, so free memory
' alone reports a healthy host right up to the point it is thrashing. Swap delta
' is the signal; free memory is not.
title Minimum requirements — what one host carries, and what it costs to exceed it

node "Operator host\n48 GiB · 14 cores" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "proveo CLI\nsbx.MemoryLimit · sbx.CPULimit" as CLI <<app>>
  component "docker daemon\nMemTotal — the VM's share, not the host's" as DOCKER <<cloud>>
}

node "sandboxd" as DAEMON <<cloud>> {
  component "auto-stop\n30s after last session" as STOP <<cloud>>
}

database "sandbox VM 1\n-m share · --cpus share" as VM1 <<db>>
database "sandbox VM n\n-m share · --cpus share" as VMN <<db>>
component "host exhaustion\nswap · SIGKILL 137 · no OOMKilled flag" as OOM <<error>>

CLI ARROW_MAIN DOCKER : MemTotal, bounded at 10s
DOCKER ARROW_MAIN CLI : the number sbx cannot see
CLI ARROW_MAIN VM1 : -m 8192m --cpus 7
CLI ARROW_MAIN VMN : the SAME share, n times over
VM1 ARROW_QUEUE DAEMON
VMN ARROW_QUEUE DAEMON
VMN ARROW_ERROR OOM : n x share > host
OOM ARROW_ERROR VM1 : a neighbour's VM is what dies

note right of CLI
  **Three knobs, and a default that moves for nobody.**

  ""PROVEO_SBX_MEMORY"" — the cap outright ("8g", "8192m",
  bytes). Skips the derivation, so it is the only one that
  works when the daemon cannot be read at all.

  ""PROVEO_SBX_CPUS"" — likewise for cores, and the only
  way to pin an exact count.

  ""PROVEO_SBX_INSTANCES"" — how many are MEANT to coexist.
  Divides MemTotal so the shares sum to the machine rather
  than to a multiple of it. It does NOT divide the CPUs:
  it only signals that several will coexist, and the CPU
  share is then half the machine whatever n is.

  A malformed value falls BACK to the derivation rather
  than through to sbx: sbx rejects a bad ""-m"" once the run
  is under way and names a flag, never the variable behind
  it. Same reason ""Available"" refuses an old CLI instead of
  trying it.
end note

note bottom of OOM
  **The arithmetic nothing refuses.** Each limit binds on its
  own, so N sandboxes may each hold a valid limit while the
  set is impossible. No component sees the sum: sbx sizes one
  sandbox, the daemon reports one total, the guest counts
  only itself.

  ""tests/e2e/capacity_test\.go"" — the capacity probe — is
  the only thing that measures the sum, by launching
  sandboxes until the host says no and reporting WHERE it
  said it.
end note

note bottom of STOP
  **Idle instances still hold their share.** auto-stop fires
  30s after the last session disconnects, not when the agent
  goes quiet — so a sandbox sitting at an idle prompt is a
  full allocation, and "how many can I run" counts the ones
  nobody is typing into.
end note
@enduml
